/* LISTENING — Listen and Choose a Response (lot B : travail, services, banque, vie sociale, voyages, santé, achats et logement) */
(function () {
  CONTENT.lcr.push(
    { id: 'lzb001', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'The tracking page says delivered, but there\'s nothing on my porch, and frankly, I\'m beginning to suspect the driver\'s having a laugh.', o: ['I\'m glad the driver is in such a good mood.', 'Please look around your porch more carefully before you laugh.', 'Tracking is available on the page for up to thirty days.', 'I understand, sir. I can open a lost-parcel claim right now.'], a: 3, x: '« Have a laugh » = se moquer de quelqu\'un. La réponse du service client doit proposer une solution. A et B manquent d\'empathie ou prennent l\'expression au sens littéral.' },
    { id: 'lzb002', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m just browsing, thanks.', o: ['Yes, I\'m browsing the web as well.', 'Thanks, I\'ll have the same, please.', 'No problem. Let me know if you need anything.', 'I browse the fridge when I\'m hungry.'], a: 2, x: '« I\'m just browsing » = je regarde seulement. Le vendeur répond poliment. Les autres jouent sur « browse ».' },
    { id: 'lzb003', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'That\'s a lot of overtime for a project that was supposed to wrap up in June.', o: ['Tell me about it. The scope kept creeping up on us.', 'Yes, I\'ll wrap it up and mail it in June.', 'Overtime is paid at one and a half times the rate.', 'June was warmer than usual this year.'], a: 0, x: '« Wrap up » = terminer ; commentaire désabusé sur un projet qui dérape. A répète « wrap up » sans comprendre ; B et C sont hors sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb004', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m calling to see where things stand with my claim.', o: ['Things stand on the table by the entrance.', 'Let me pull it up. It looks like it\'s still with the assessor.', 'I haven\'t claimed anything since last spring.', 'You\'ll stand to benefit from calling earlier.'], a: 1, x: '« Where things stand » = où en est-on. Les pièges réutilisent « stand » et « claim » dans d\'autres sens.' },
    { id: 'lzb005', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Is this the right platform for the express to Manchester?', o: ['No, you want platform nine, just over the footbridge.', 'It\'s a right turn at the end of the express.', 'Yes, the Manchester platform is expensive.', 'I took the express train to Manchester last week.'], a: 0, x: 'Question oui/non avec correction. Les pièges répètent « right », « express » et le thème sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb006', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Rest and fluids. That\'s all the doctor prescribed.', o: ['Prescribed rest is available at any pharmacy.', 'I\'ll have a double, with plenty of ice.', 'The doctor said all of that on the phone.', 'Sounds like a bug that\'s going around. Do take it easy.'], a: 3, x: '« A bug going around » = un virus qui circule. La bonne réponse déduit le diagnostic. Les autres sont hors sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb007', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'Honestly, the open-plan layout looks lovely in the photos, but I\'m not sure we\'d ever get a moment\'s peace.', o: ['Peace is the main reason I moved to the country.', 'I see your point. With the kids, I\'d want a door I could close.', 'The photos are lovely, but I\'d prefer a bigger room.', 'A moment is all it takes to take a photo, honestly.'], a: 1, x: 'Réserve exprimée de manière indirecte (« I\'m not sure we\'d ever get… »). La bonne réponse adhère. Les autres répètent « peace », « photos ».' },
    { id: 'lzb008', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Could you cover my shift on Saturday? I\'ll return the favor.', o: ['Thanks, I\'ll return it to you on Saturday.', 'The Saturday schedule is posted outside the break room.', 'I can\'t this week, but try Dana. She was looking for extra hours.', 'You\'re welcome, I\'m glad I could cover for you.'], a: 2, x: '« Cover my shift » = me remplacer. Les pièges inversent les rôles (c\'est l\'interlocuteur qui demande) ou ignorent la demande. La bonne réponse refuse poliment et propose une alternative.' },
    { id: 'lzb009', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I\'ve never been so happy to see a taxi in my life.', o: ['I bet. Was the walk from the station as long as it looked?', 'Taxis are cheaper than they used to be.', 'I\'ve never been in a taxi in my life.', 'Then you should have waited for the bus.'], a: 0, x: 'Hyperbole exprimant le soulagement. La bonne réponse en tire l\'inférence (longue marche). Les autres répètent « taxi » sans comprendre.' },
    { id: 'lzb010', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'Not to put too fine a point on it, but if we miss this deadline, heads will roll.', o: ['That\'s an awful thing to say. Nobody is going to get hurt.', 'Let\'s put a finer point on it in tomorrow\'s budget meeting.', 'Don\'t worry, I\'ll roll up the posters and take them to the head office.', 'I know, which is why I\'m asking everyone to double up this week.'], a: 3, x: '« Heads will roll » = il y aura des sanctions (idiome). A le prend au pied de la lettre ; B et C répètent « fine point » et « roll/head » hors sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb011', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'He\'s been in and out of the hospital all year, poor man, and he\'s still cracking jokes.', o: ['I hope he has a hospital bed by the window.', 'That takes some spirit. I hope he\'s on the mend.', 'He has been cracking eggs all year, I suppose.', 'Yes, he was wise to stay out of the hospital.'], a: 1, x: '« In and out of hospital » = hospitalisé à répétition. La bonne réponse admire son courage. Les pièges prennent les expressions littéralement ou contredisent.' },
    { id: 'lzb012', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Did the new hire work out, or is it still too early to tell?', o: ['It\'s still too early for the shop to open, I\'m afraid.', 'Yes, he worked out the schedule on his own.', 'It\'s a bit early to say, but she\'s picked things up faster than I expected.', 'He told me the hiring process took three months.'], a: 2, x: 'Question « ou bien » : réponse nuancée (trop tôt, mais bon début). « Work out » signifie ici « donner satisfaction » ; les pièges l\'utilisent dans un autre sens ou changent de sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb013', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I\'d hoped to have the budget figures to you by now, but accounting has been sitting on the numbers.', o: ['Then please sit down and we\'ll go through them together.', 'No worries. Just forward them to me as soon as you get them.', 'I\'d hoped so, too, but the deadline was yesterday.', 'The figures are on the third floor, in the budget office.'], a: 1, x: '« Sit on something » = garder sans agir. Le piège A prend l\'expression au pied de la lettre. B accuse alors que l\'on doit simplement réagir à l\'excuse ; C ignore la situation.' },
    { id: 'lzb014', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'The last time I flew with this airline, I was rebooked twice, and my luggage took a detour through Lisbon.', o: ['That doesn\'t exactly inspire confidence, does it?', 'Lisbon is lovely in the spring. I\'d go back anytime.', 'Twice is the maximum they allow for one reservation.', 'I\'d take a detour to Lisbon myself if the fare were low.'], a: 0, x: 'Le locuteur se plaint avec ironie (« took a detour »). La bonne réponse fait écho à son jugement négatif. Les autres ignorent la plainte.' },
    { id: 'lzb015', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'We\'re looking at a two-hour delay, give or take, and they\'re not promising anything beyond that.', o: ['Good, we\'ll be in the air within the hour, then.', 'Take it or leave it. They\'re not offering any compensation.', 'Ugh. Might as well get something to eat while we wait.', 'Beyond that is where the gate is, I think.'], a: 2, x: '« Give or take » = environ. La bonne réponse réagit au retard avec résignation. A comprend le contraire.' },
    { id: 'lzb016', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Would it be possible to waive the overdraft fee just this once?', o: ['The overdraft was on my account last week.', 'I\'d waive my right to a refund, too.', 'Once a month is what the fee costs.', 'Since it\'s your first one this year, I think I can do that.'], a: 3, x: 'Demande polie de geste commercial → accord conditionnel. Les autres répètent « overdraft », « waive », « once ».' },
    { id: 'lzb017', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ve been mulling over handing in my notice.', o: ['Yes, I handed in my report on time.', 'I noticed that, too. It was on the notice board.', 'Thanks, I\'ll pass it on to the whole team.', 'Wow, that\'s a big step. Is something going on, or is it just the commute?'], a: 3, x: '« Hand in one\'s notice » = démissionner. La bonne réponse réagit à la confidence. Les pièges jouent sur « notice » (affichage, remarquer) et « hand in ».' },
    { id: 'lzb018', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I wouldn\'t say Tom is difficult to work with, exactly. He just has strong views on everything.', o: ['Then he sounds like the easiest colleague you have.', 'Ah, so he can be a bit of a handful, then.', 'I\'d say so, too. He never takes a stand on anything.', 'Which views? I haven\'t read the survey yet.'], a: 1, x: 'Euphémisme : « I wouldn\'t say… exactly… just » adoucit un jugement négatif. Il faut lire entre les lignes : Tom est un peu pénible. A et B vont à l\'opposé du sens sous-entendu.' },
    { id: 'lzb019', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I\'d have invited her, but I didn\'t want things to get awkward with Jake.', o: ['Fair enough, though she might hear about it from someone else.', 'Great, so she\'ll be coming to the party, then.', 'I\'ve never invited anybody without telling Jake.', 'Next time, she\'ll have invited him, I\'m sure.'], a: 0, x: 'Conditionnel passé (« I\'d have invited ») : elle n\'a PAS été invitée. La bonne réponse comprend cela. A contredit ; B et C n\'ont pas de cohérence logique.' },
    { id: 'lzb020', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'Her recovery has been nothing short of remarkable, considering what she went through.', o: ['Short of what? I didn\'t hear what she went through.', 'Yes, it\'s a shame she didn\'t make it.', 'Absolutely. The surgeons were expecting months, not weeks.', 'She should go through the exercises slowly.'], a: 2, x: '« Nothing short of remarkable » = vraiment remarquable. La bonne réponse confirme avec un détail. B contredit, A et C dévient.' },
    { id: 'lzb021', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'Her portfolio took a real beating when the market turned.', o: ['She should take care. Beating people is against the law.', 'Her portfolio is about the size of a briefcase, I think.', 'Poor thing. But she\'s young, so she has time to ride it out.', 'Good for her. The market must be turning in her favor.'], a: 2, x: '« Take a beating » = subir de lourdes pertes. La bonne réponse compatit. A est littéral ; C comprend le contraire.' },
    { id: 'lzb022', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'If I\'d known it was fancy dress, I wouldn\'t have come within a mile of the place.', o: ['Yes, a mile is about how far it is from here.', 'I\'d have worn the pirate costume if I\'d known it was fancy.', 'Fancy dress is what I\'d call the prices in that restaurant.', 'Ha! You\'d have hated it. Half the room is dressed as pirates.'], a: 3, x: 'Conditionnel de troisième type : il ne savait pas, et il est venu. La bonne réponse prolonge l\'humour. Les pièges reprennent « mile » et « fancy ».' },
    { id: 'lzb023', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'The doctor wants to run a few tests, just to rule things out.', o: ['That sounds sensible. Did she say how long the results would take?', 'Running is the best way to rule out illness.', 'Yes, tests ruled the school last year.', 'I ran into her at the clinic this morning.'], a: 0, x: '« Rule out » = écarter. La bonne réponse réagit et pose une question logique. Les pièges jouent sur « run » et « rule ».' },
    { id: 'lzb024', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'Is delivery included, or do I have to arrange it myself?', o: ['I arranged the flowers on the table.', 'Delivery is free for orders over a hundred dollars.', 'Yes, I included it in my budget.', 'The order was delivered on Monday morning.'], a: 1, x: 'Question « ou bien » → condition. Les autres répètent « arrange », « included ».' },
    { id: 'lzb025', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Remind me, how do you two know each other?', o: ['We know each other\'s names, as you can see.', 'Yes, I remind her every Monday.', 'We were flatmates years ago. She was the tidy one.', 'Two years ago, I\'d have known her anywhere.'], a: 2, x: '« How do you know each other » → réponse sur l\'origine de la relation. Les pièges jouent sur « remind » et « know ».' },
    { id: 'lzb026', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'My doctor says I should cut back on caffeine.', o: ['That must be rough. You practically live on espresso.', 'Then you should cut back on sleep instead of coffee.', 'Caffeine is found in tea and chocolate as well.', 'I back her up. The doctor is always right.'], a: 0, x: '« Cut back on » = réduire. La bonne réponse compatit avec un commentaire personnel. Les pièges jouent sur « cut » et « back ».' },
    { id: 'lzb027', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Are you up for pulling an all-nighter to get this proposal out?', o: ['No, I pulled it out of the folder last night.', 'Yes, the proposal went out on Monday.', 'I\'m up already. It\'s almost ten o\'clock.', 'I\'d rather not, but if it\'s the only way, I\'m in.'], a: 3, x: '« Up for » = partant pour ; « all-nighter » = nuit blanche. Les pièges jouent sur « pull out », « up » et « proposal » sans répondre à la question.' },
    { id: 'lzb028', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'The neighbors upstairs are driving me up the wall.', o: ['The wall is too high for the stairs.', 'That sounds exhausting. Have you spoken to them or the landlord?', 'They\'ve driven up from the south this morning.', 'Upstairs is where the paint is stored.'], a: 1, x: '« Drive someone up the wall » = exaspérer. Les pièges prennent l\'expression littéralement.' },
    { id: 'lzb029', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m having a few friends over on Saturday. Do you want to come?', o: ['Over Saturday is the best time for the party.', 'Yes, I have a few friends, too.', 'I\'d love to. What can I bring?', 'I came over to Saturday\'s last week.'], a: 2, x: 'Invitation → acceptation + question pratique. Les autres répètent « friends », « Saturday ».' },
    { id: 'lzb030', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'m looking to consolidate my loans into a single payment.', o: ['Yes, the single room is a bit costly.', 'I\'d pay the loan off in a single month.', 'We consolidate all the offices on Fridays.', 'That can make sense. Let me pull up your balances and compare rates.'], a: 3, x: '« Consolidate loans » = regrouper des prêts. Les pièges répètent « single » ou « consolidate » dans un autre contexte.' },
    { id: 'lzb031', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'Do I need an appointment to meet with a financial advisor?', o: ['Walk-ins are welcome, but an appointment will save you waiting.', 'Yes, I advised her to meet at noon.', 'The financial office is a ten-minute walk.', 'I need a pen to sign the paper.'], a: 0, x: 'Question oui/non sur la nécessité → réponse nuancée. Les autres répètent « advisor », « financial » ou « meet » sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb032', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'d like to set up a standing order for my rent.', o: ['Standing orders are a bit hard for my back.', 'Of course. I\'ll need the landlord\'s account number and the amount.', 'I rent a flat near the station.', 'Yes, I\'ll stand in line for you.'], a: 1, x: 'Standing order = virement permanent. La bonne réponse demande les informations nécessaires. Les pièges jouent sur « stand » et « rent ».' },
    { id: 'lzb033', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'The place is a bit of a fixer-upper, but the price is right.', o: ['Sounds promising, as long as the foundations are sound.', 'Right, the fixer is up the stairs.', 'Yes, the price is on the right side of the shop.', 'I\'m too old to fix the upper floors.'], a: 0, x: '« Fixer-upper » = logement à rénover. La bonne réponse nuance l\'enthousiasme. Les pièges répètent « fix », « right ».' },
    { id: 'lzb034', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Would it be all right if I left a bit early today? I have a doctor\'s appointment.', o: ['Yes, early is always better than late.', 'Of course. Just make sure your calls are forwarded.', 'I have a doctor as well, but he\'s in Boston.', 'The appointment lasts about an hour, I think.'], a: 1, x: 'Demande de permission → accord et condition. Les autres réponses répètent « early », « doctor », « appointment » sans accorder la permission.' },
    { id: 'lzb035', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I\'d like to return these shoes. They gave me blisters.', o: ['They are blistering hot on the shoe shelf.', 'The shoes were returned from the manufacturer.', 'Sorry to hear that. Do you have the receipt, and were they worn outdoors?', 'Gave you the blisters? Then you\'ve worn them well.'], a: 2, x: 'Demande de retour → le vendeur pose les questions d\'usage. Les pièges jouent sur « blister » et « return ».' },
    { id: 'lzb036', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I wasn\'t blown away by the showroom, but the sales guy certainly knew how to lay it on thick.', o: ['Blown away by the wind? I hope the showroom survived.', 'Yes, he laid the carpet on thick, and it looked great.', 'Yes, I was blown away by the sales guy, too.', 'Ha, I know the type. Did you sign anything?'], a: 3, x: '« Lay it on thick » = exagérer les compliments. La bonne réponse comprend l\'expression. Les pièges sont littéraux ou contredisent.' },
    { id: 'lzb037', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Do you fancy a game of tennis this weekend, weather permitting?', o: ['The weather permit is in my wallet.', 'Sounds good, as long as it doesn\'t rain like last week.', 'I fancied her last weekend, to be honest.', 'Yes, I played tennis last weekend.'], a: 1, x: 'Invitation → acceptation conditionnelle. Les pièges jouent sur « weather permit », « fancy » et le mauvais temps verbal.' },
    { id: 'lzb038', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I don\'t mean to be a wet blanket, but the figures in the forecast look awfully rosy.', o: ['I know. I\'d say we should stress-test them against a downturn.', 'It\'s not raining, so there\'s no need for a blanket.', 'Pink does look good on the front page, I agree.', 'Rosy is how I\'d describe the weather on Thursday.'], a: 0, x: '« Wet blanket » = rabat-joie ; « rosy figures » = prévisions trop optimistes. La bonne réponse propose un test de résistance. Les autres sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb039', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I\'d like to dispute a charge on my statement. I don\'t recognize the merchant.', o: ['I recognize that merchant. He sells vegetables.', 'Statements are mailed on the twelfth of each month.', 'Certainly. I\'ll flag it and issue a temporary credit while we investigate.', 'Your charge is being disputed by the bank\'s manager.'], a: 2, x: 'Contester un prélèvement → la banque ouvre une enquête. Les autres réponses ignorent la demande ou renversent les rôles.' },
    { id: 'lzb040', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'Tell me you booked a refundable fare, because the forecast is looking dicey.', o: ['I\'d tell you, but the fare is already booked.', 'Dicey is the wrong word. It\'s the cheapest fare.', 'I booked a refund, so you can have your dice back.', 'Luckily I did. I\'ve got until midnight to change it for free.'], a: 3, x: '« Dicey » = risqué. « Tell me you… » = j\'espère que tu as. La bonne réponse confirme. Les autres sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb041', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ve been meaning to get in touch, but time just got away from me.', o: ['Next time, please set an alarm so time doesn\'t get away.', 'I got in touch with her, too, two weeks ago.', 'Don\'t give it a thought. I\'m just glad you called.', 'You should have told me before it was too late to get in.'], a: 2, x: 'Excuse typique : « time got away from me ». La bonne réponse est généreuse et naturelle. Les pièges répètent « time », « get in ».' },
    { id: 'lzb042', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I hear you and Priya have fallen out.', o: ['We have, over something silly, but I\'m hoping to patch things up.', 'No, I fell over on the ice, but I\'m all right.', 'The leaves have fallen out of the garden.', 'Priya and I will go out on Saturday.'], a: 0, x: '« Fall out » = se brouiller. A prend l\'expression au sens littéral ; B et C n\'ont pas de lien.' },
    { id: 'lzb043', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'We\'re on the waiting list for a two-bedroom, but it could be months.', o: ['Yes, the bedroom is too small for two.', 'Waiting lists are a thing of the past.', 'Months pass quickly when you\'re on a list.', 'That\'s a long time. Have you thought of looking in the suburbs?'], a: 3, x: 'La bonne réponse offre une alternative. Les autres contredisent ou répètent les mots.' },
    { id: 'lzb044', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'m afraid I\'ve double-booked myself on Friday.', o: ['Yes, I\'ve booked a table for two on Friday.', 'No worries, we can do dinner some other night.', 'Afraid of what? There\'s nothing to fear on Friday.', 'I read two books last Friday, too.'], a: 1, x: '« I\'m afraid » est une formule de politesse (pas de la peur) annonçant un empêchement. A et C ne répondent pas.' },
    { id: 'lzb045', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I was told someone would call me back yesterday. Nobody did.', o: ['It was yesterday, so I\'m calling you back now.', 'Nobody told me to call. My phone was out of battery.', 'I apologize for that. I\'ll make a note and call you myself within the hour.', 'Yes, somebody would have been told to call the number.'], a: 2, x: 'Plainte avec rappel des faits → excuse et engagement. A est illogique ; B est défensif ; C est du blabla au conditionnel passé.' },
    { id: 'lzb046', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'It\'s not exactly my cup of tea, but I\'ll come along for moral support.', o: ['Thanks, I owe you one. I\'ll make sure you get a decent seat, at least.', 'I\'ll put the kettle on when we get there.', 'Moral support is the best kind of tea.', 'Tea\'s not my cup, either, but I\'ll take a coffee.'], a: 0, x: '« Not my cup of tea » = pas mon genre. La bonne réponse remercie et rassure. Les pièges poursuivent le thème du thé littéralement.' },
    { id: 'lzb047', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I wouldn\'t touch that place with a ten-foot pole. The reviews are terrible.', o: ['A ten-foot pole is hard to find in this store.', 'Fair point. Let\'s find somewhere with a better track record.', 'The reviews are made by the owner\'s mother.', 'Touching the place is not allowed, I\'m afraid.'], a: 1, x: '« Not touch with a ten-foot pole » = éviter à tout prix. La bonne réponse approuve et propose une alternative. Les pièges sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb048', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Would you mind if I reclined my seat a little?', o: ['No, I don\'t think the seat is mine.', 'Yes, I\'ve been mindful of the legroom.', 'I declined his offer of a free upgrade.', 'Not at all, go ahead. I\'ll move my laptop back a bit.'], a: 3, x: '« Would you mind if… » : « Not at all » = d\'accord. Les pièges ne répondent pas ou jouent sur « recline/decline ».' },
    { id: 'lzb049', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Is the deposit refundable if we decide not to go ahead with the lease?', o: ['The deposit was left on the table.', 'We go ahead of the lease by two weeks.', 'Leasing is cheaper than buying in general.', 'Only partly, I\'m afraid. The administration fee is non-refundable.'], a: 3, x: 'Question sur les conditions du dépôt → réponse nuancée. Les autres répètent « deposit », « lease ».' },
    { id: 'lzb050', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Could you tell me how to update the billing address on my account?', o: ['Sure. Just log in and go to account settings, or I can do it for you now.', 'Yes, the address is on Maple Street.', 'I update my account every few days.', 'Billing happens on the first of every month.'], a: 0, x: 'Question « how » → explication de la procédure. Les autres réponses parlent d\'adresse, de compte ou de facturation sans expliquer.' },
    { id: 'lzb051', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Excuse me, I\'ve been waiting here for a while. Are you serving this line?', o: ['Yes, I\'ve been served by the waiter.', 'Sorry about that. I\'ll be right with you.', 'The line is very long today.', 'It\'s a while since I was last here.'], a: 1, x: 'Client impatient → excuse et promesse de s\'occuper de lui. Les autres répètent « waiting », « line », « while » sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb052', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I could do with a night out, to be honest.', o: ['You could do with a night\'s sleep before the trip.', 'Out of what? I didn\'t catch what you wanted to do.', 'Say no more. I know a place that has live music on Thursdays.', 'I could do without it, honestly. Nights are for sleep.'], a: 2, x: '« I could do with » = j\'aurais bien besoin de. La bonne réponse comprend l\'invitation indirecte et propose quelque chose. A et B déforment les mots ; C inverse le sens.' },
    { id: 'lzb053', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'We were going to rent a car, but it turns out parking in the old town is a nightmare.', o: ['Nightmares are common when you travel a lot.', 'I\'d skip the car, then. The trams cover most of it.', 'Then you should rent a bigger car for the old town.', 'Yes, cars are for rent in the new town.'], a: 1, x: 'Conseil pratique en réponse à une difficulté. Les pièges répètent « rent » et « car ».' },
    { id: 'lzb054', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'These jeans are a bit tight. Do you have the next size up?', o: ['Yes, they\'re very tight on my budget.', 'I\'ve gone up a size since the holidays.', 'Let me check the back. I think we have a thirty-four.', 'They look great on you, and they\'re on sale.'], a: 2, x: 'Question de disponibilité → vérification. Les autres répètent « tight » et « size » sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb055', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'Which exit should I take for the airport?', o: ['The airport is rather busy this time of year.', 'Yes, I exited from the front door.', 'There are twelve exits on this highway.', 'Take exit twelve, then follow the signs.'], a: 3, x: '« Which » → un choix précis. Les autres répètent « airport », « exit » sans donner le numéro.' },
    { id: 'lzb056', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'She\'s been passed over for promotion twice now.', o: ['That has to sting, especially after all the extra hours she\'s put in.', 'Yes, she passed the exam twice, too.', 'The promotion is being held in the main auditorium.', 'I\'d pass it over to her, if I were you.'], a: 0, x: '« Be passed over » = être écarté d\'une promotion. La réponse montre l\'empathie attendue. Les pièges jouent sur « pass » et « promotion » sans comprendre l\'expression.' },
    { id: 'lzb057', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'The delivery was supposed to arrive between nine and noon. It\'s nearly four.', o: ['I\'m so sorry about that. Let me track the driver and see where he\'s got to.', 'Between nine and noon is the busiest time of day.', 'It\'s nearly four o\'clock, so we\'re about to close.', 'Supposedly, but I\'d arrive at four if I were you.'], a: 0, x: 'Plainte → excuse + action. Les pièges reprennent les heures sans traiter le retard.' },
    { id: 'lzb058', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'Don\'t look now, but your ex just walked in.', o: ['I\'ll look in a minute, once the bill is settled.', 'Great. Any chance we could sneak out the back?', 'He\'s been walking in this neighborhood for years.', 'I\'m not looking. I\'ll just walk in later.'], a: 1, x: '« Don\'t look now, but… » = ne te retourne pas. La bonne réponse comprend la situation gênante. Les autres répètent « look/walk in ».' },
    { id: 'lzb059', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'If I\'m reading the tea leaves right, there\'s a hiring freeze coming.', o: ['I prefer coffee, honestly. Tea makes me jittery.', 'Where did you read that? I haven\'t seen the leaves.', 'That would explain why they\'ve gone so quiet about the open positions.', 'Then I\'ll freeze the agenda until the end of the month.'], a: 2, x: '« Read the tea leaves » = deviner à partir d\'indices. La bonne réponse relie l\'hypothèse à un fait observé. A et B sont littéraux ; C répète « freeze » seulement.' },
    { id: 'lzb060', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I don\'t suppose you\'d have a few minutes to go over the Henderson account?', o: ['No, I don\'t suppose I would either.', 'The Henderson account was closed down last quarter, I think.', 'I haven\'t been to their offices, to be honest.', 'Sure, let me just finish this email and I\'ll swing by your desk.'], a: 3, x: '« I don\'t suppose you\'d… » est une demande très polie et indirecte : on répond par un accord concret. Pièges : répondre au sens littéral de « suppose » ou parler du dossier sans répondre à la demande.' },
    { id: 'lzb061', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'Not that I\'m complaining, but the upgrade to business class came out of nowhere.', o: ['I\'m complaining, too. The upgrade is out of order.', 'Nowhere is a place I\'d rather not visit.', 'Complaints go through the airline\'s website, not the aircrew.', 'Perks of the job, I guess. Or someone upstairs owes you a favor.'], a: 3, x: '« Not that I\'m complaining » = je ne m\'en plains pas. La bonne réponse spécule sur la raison. Les pièges jouent sur « complain » et « nowhere ».' },
    { id: 'lzb062', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Is it worth getting the rail pass, or should I just buy tickets as I go?', o: ['It\'s worth about ninety dollars, as far as I know.', 'I passed the station as I was going.', 'If you\'re doing more than three trips, the pass will pay for itself.', 'As I go, I always buy tickets at the gate.'], a: 2, x: 'Question « ou bien » → conseil conditionnel. Les autres réponses répètent « worth », « pass », « go ».' },
    { id: 'lzb063', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Does the apartment come furnished, or would we be starting from scratch?', o: ['It\'s semi-furnished: there\'s a bed and a sofa, but no table.', 'I scratched the table when we moved in.', 'Furnishing is expensive at the start.', 'It\'s on the second floor, past the stairs.'], a: 0, x: '« Start from scratch » = repartir de zéro. La bonne réponse nuance. Les pièges répètent « scratch » et « furnished ».' },
    { id: 'lzb064', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'Well, that presentation went about as smoothly as a root canal.', o: ['Thanks, I worked on it for weeks, so I\'m glad it showed.', 'Tell me about it. The projector died, and then the file wouldn\'t even open.', 'I\'d see a dentist about that, if I were you.', 'Then let\'s hope the next one goes even more smoothly.'], a: 1, x: 'Comparaison ironique : « smooth as a root canal » signifie que ce fut pénible. Les pièges prennent la phrase comme un compliment (A, D) ou au sens médical littéral (B).' },
    { id: 'lzb065', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Would you like to join us for a game night on Friday?', o: ['Yes, the game was on Friday night.', 'That sounds fun, but I\'ve got a family dinner that night.', 'I would like it if you\'d join us.', 'Friday nights are good for the family.'], a: 1, x: 'Invitation → refus poli avec explication. Les autres répètent les mots sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb066', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'She\'s got a way of making everyone feel at home.', o: ['Yes, she\'s at home on weekends.', 'I don\'t know the way to her house, either.', 'She really does. It\'s hard not to like her.', 'Everyone feels at home in a house this size.'], a: 2, x: '« A way of doing » = un don pour. La bonne réponse approuve le compliment. Les pièges répètent « way » et « home » littéralement.' },
    { id: 'lzb067', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'d budgeted for the sofa, not for the delivery fee and the assembly charge on top.', o: ['Those extras add up. Maybe ask them to waive one.', 'Yes, assembly is on top of the stairs.', 'The sofa is in budget, so you can sit on it.', 'Charge your phone before the delivery arrives.'], a: 0, x: '« On top » = en plus. La bonne réponse conseille. Les pièges jouent sur « charge » et « top ».' },
    { id: 'lzb068', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'To be fair, the physio did warn me not to overdo it, and what did I do? Played two sets of tennis.', o: ['To be fair, two sets is not enough for a game.', 'I did warn you, and you were right to play tennis.', 'Physios do overdo it. They charge too much.', 'You\'d think we\'d learn. Is the knee swollen?'], a: 3, x: 'Question rhétorique autocritique. La bonne réponse compatit et pose une question utile. Les pièges la contredisent.' },
    { id: 'lzb069', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Could you walk me through the cancellation fees one more time? I\'m afraid I\'m still not following.', o: ['Of course. If you cancel within forty-eight hours, it\'s thirty percent.', 'I\'m following you on social media, too.', 'Sure, I\'ll walk you to the door in a moment.', 'One more time and the fees will be cancelled.'], a: 0, x: '« Walk me through » = m\'expliquer étape par étape. Les pièges prennent « walk » et « follow » littéralement.' },
    { id: 'lzb070', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Could we take this offline? I don\'t want to derail the meeting.', o: ['Sure, I\'ll disconnect my laptop from the network.', 'The train derailed near the station, as far as I know.', 'Good call. Let\'s pick it up after everyone\'s gone.', 'No, I don\'t want to lose the connection either.'], a: 2, x: '« Take it offline » = en discuter à part. Les pièges prennent « offline » (déconnecté) et « derail » (train) au sens littéral.' },
    { id: 'lzb071', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'The loan officer said I\'d need a co-signer.', o: ['Yes, officers sign for a coffee, too.', 'That\'s pretty common when you haven\'t built up much credit yet.', 'Common names are easy to sign, but not the long ones.', 'Then the loan must have been approved without any help.'], a: 1, x: 'Co-signer = garant. La bonne réponse explique la raison habituelle. Les autres jouent sur « sign » et inversent l\'information.' },
    { id: 'lzb072', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Do you have anything for a sore throat?', o: ['Yes, I have a sore foot, too.', 'Yes, a sore throat is common in the winter.', 'Nothing is for sale after nine.', 'These lozenges work well, and you can have tea with honey.'], a: 3, x: 'Question à un pharmacien → une recommandation. Les autres répètent « sore » ou « anything ».' },
    { id: 'lzb073', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'We should do this more often.', o: ['We did it more often last year, I think.', 'Yes, I did it more than you suppose.', 'Absolutely. Let\'s put something in the calendar before we lose track.', 'Often is how I\'d describe the restaurant\'s menu.'], a: 2, x: '« We should do this more often » est une proposition indirecte. La bonne réponse l\'accepte et fait avancer le projet. Les autres répètent « more often » sans s\'engager.' },
    { id: 'lzb074', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'Do you know who\'s in charge of ordering office supplies now?', o: ['Yes, we order supplies on Mondays.', 'I think it\'s Marcus, since Ana moved to the Berlin office.', 'The supplies are in the closet on the left.', 'Ana is charging her phone in the office.'], a: 1, x: '« Who » appelle un nom. A et B répondent à d\'autres questions (quand, où) ; C répète « charge » et « office » sans rapport.' },
    { id: 'lzb075', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'How much is the rent, and are utilities included?', o: ['The rent is due on the first of the month.', 'Utilities are very useful in an apartment.', 'I rented it from the landlord last year.', 'It\'s twelve hundred a month, and water and heating are included.'], a: 3, x: 'Double question → montant et inclusions. Les autres répondent partiellement ou pas du tout.' },
    { id: 'lzb076', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ll be honest, I\'m not sure I\'m cut out for client-facing work.', o: ['Give it time. Nobody is good at it in their first few months.', 'Then you should have cut the client meeting from the schedule.', 'Thanks, I\'ll be honest with the client from now on.', 'You\'re right. It\'s better to stay on the client\'s side of the desk.'], a: 0, x: '« Be cut out for » = avoir les qualités pour. La réponse rassure. Les pièges répètent « cut », « honest », « client » sans répondre à l\'inquiétude.' },
    { id: 'lzb077', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I really should get going, but this has been lovely.', o: ['Yes, you should go. You\'ve been here for hours.', 'Lovely weather we\'ve been having this week.', 'It\'s been great having you. Let\'s not leave it so long next time.', 'It\'s a long way, so you\'d better leave early.'], a: 2, x: 'Formule de départ polie → réponse chaleureuse. A est impoli (registre inadapté) ; B et C changent de sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb078', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'The rent has gone up again. At this rate, we\'ll be priced out of the neighborhood.', o: ['Yes, prices are out of the neighborhood, so I shop at the supermarket.', 'It\'s happening everywhere. Maybe it\'s time to look further out.', 'At this rate, I\'ll be there in ten minutes.', 'The neighborhood has a park and a library.'], a: 1, x: '« Priced out » = chassé par les prix. La bonne réponse conseille. Les pièges répètent « rate » et « neighborhood ».' },
    { id: 'lzb079', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ll level with you: at this rate, we\'ll be in the red by the end of the quarter.', o: ['Then we\'d better look at where we can cut costs before the board meets.', 'Good. A profit by the end of the quarter is just what we need.', 'I\'ll level the floor myself before the quarter ends.', 'Red or blue, the paperwork has to be done by Friday.'], a: 0, x: '« In the red » = en déficit ; « level with » = être franc. A comprend le contraire ; B et C jouent sur « level » et « red » hors sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb080', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I hate to be a pain, but would it be at all possible to have a different room? The one you\'ve given us backs onto the elevator.', o: ['Yes, of course. The back rooms are the best on the floor.', 'I\'m afraid the elevators are for staff and luggage only.', 'It\'s possible, but the pain should go away by itself.', 'Not a pain at all, sir. Let me see what we have on a quieter floor.'], a: 3, x: 'Demande très atténuée (« I hate to be a pain… at all possible »). La réponse rassure puis propose une solution. « Pain » dans C est pris au sens médical ; A contredit la plainte.' },
    { id: 'lzb081', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'We\'ve drifted apart since she moved to Seattle.', o: ['Yes, drifting is dangerous on the highway.', 'Seattle is a rainy place, as far as I know.', 'Seattle apartments are expensive. I\'ve seen the listings.', 'That happens. Distance makes it hard to keep up.'], a: 3, x: '« Drift apart » = s\'éloigner l\'un de l\'autre. La bonne réponse compatit. Les pièges prennent l\'expression littéralement ou changent de sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb082', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Do you reckon we\'ll make our connection?', o: ['It\'ll be tight, but if we hurry through security, we should just about.', 'We connect the lines through the phone.', 'Yes, we made the reservation online.', 'I reckon the connection is about three hundred miles.'], a: 0, x: '« Make a connection » = ne pas rater la correspondance. Les pièges changent le sens de « connection ».' },
    { id: 'lzb083', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ve been burning the midnight oil, and it\'s starting to catch up with me.', o: ['Midnight oil is cheaper than it used to be.', 'Yes, I\'ll catch up with you at the station.', 'You should get some proper sleep before you burn out completely.', 'Burning oil is dangerous without a ventilated room.'], a: 2, x: '« Burn the midnight oil » = travailler très tard. La bonne réponse conseille. Les pièges sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb084', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m really sorry, but I need to push our one-on-one back by an hour.', o: ['Sorry, I don\'t think I can push it any harder.', 'No problem. I\'ll use the time to catch up on emails.', 'One hour is a long time for a one-on-one.', 'Yes, I pushed the cart back to the hall.'], a: 1, x: '« Push back » = reporter. La bonne réponse accepte le changement. A et C prennent « push » littéralement.' },
    { id: 'lzb085', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'What\'s the plan for tonight? I\'m easy.', o: ['How about the new Thai place, then a walk by the river?', 'Yes, I\'m an easy person to cook for.', 'Tonight is the plan I made for yesterday.', 'The river is easy to cross by the bridge.'], a: 0, x: '« I\'m easy » = ça m\'est égal. La bonne réponse fait une proposition. Les autres répètent « easy », « plan ».' },
    { id: 'lzb086', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ve got an appointment at the clinic at ten. Could you drive me?', o: ['The clinic is a ten-minute walk from here.', 'Sure, I\'ll pick you up at nine thirty.', 'Yes, I\'ve got an appointment at nine.', 'I drove to the clinic yesterday.'], a: 1, x: 'Demande de service → accord + précision. Les autres répètent « clinic », « drive », « ten » sans accepter.' },
    { id: 'lzb087', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'I\'d like a round-trip ticket to Philadelphia, please.', o: ['No, it\'s a long trip to the round table.', 'I\'m going to Philadelphia on Friday, thanks.', 'The ticket is round, and the trip is long.', 'Certainly. When would you like to return?'], a: 3, x: 'Un guichetier répondrait en demandant la date de retour. Les pièges répètent les mots sans cohérence.' },
    { id: 'lzb088', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Do you take wire transfers, or is it strictly cash and check?', o: ['Wires are in the supply closet next to the cables.', 'Strictly speaking, the check bounced last week.', 'We do, though there\'s a processing fee of fifteen dollars.', 'Cash is always welcome at the front desk.'], a: 2, x: '« Wire transfer » = virement. Les pièges prennent « wire » littéralement ou répètent « cash/check » sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb089', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I won\'t hold it against you, but if the service were any slower, we\'d be going backwards.', o: ['Thank you, madam. We pride ourselves on the unhurried pace.', 'I do apologize, madam. We had two cooks call in sick tonight. Let me bring your drinks right away.', 'Going backwards isn\'t allowed in this section, madam.', 'I\'ll hold it against the kitchen, then.'], a: 1, x: 'Ironie polie d\'un client mécontent : on s\'excuse et on agit. A prend la plainte pour un compliment ; B et C jouent sur « backwards » et « hold ».' },
    { id: 'lzb090', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Do you have this in stock, or would I have to order it in?', o: ['We\'re out at the moment, but I can have it here by Thursday.', 'It\'s stocked with the winter coats upstairs, in order.', 'Yes, I\'ll have the order delivered tomorrow.', 'We take orders until closing time on weekdays.'], a: 0, x: 'Question « ou bien » → réponse précise sur le stock. Les pièges répètent « stock » et « order » sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb091', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I\'ve half a mind to tell him what I really think of his so-called strategy.', o: ['Then you\'ve got half as much to say as he does.', 'What a coincidence. I think he has half a plan, too.', 'Half of your mind is better than none, I suppose.', 'I\'d sleep on it first. You can\'t unsay it once it\'s out.'], a: 3, x: '« I\'ve half a mind to » = j\'ai bien envie de. Le conseil de patienter (« sleep on it ») est la seule réponse pertinente. A et C jouent sur « half » et « mind » littéralement.' },
    { id: 'lzb092', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'We\'re going to have to stagger lunch breaks so the phones are always covered.', o: ['Good, I\'ll book the phones for lunch.', 'Yes, it was a staggering amount of covered food.', 'Fine by me. I\'d just as soon eat later anyway.', 'Let\'s break the news to everyone at noon, then.'], a: 2, x: '« Stagger » = échelonner. La bonne réponse accepte la mesure. Les pièges : « staggering » (stupéfiant), « cover » et « break » repris hors contexte.' },
    { id: 'lzb093', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Could you hold this for me? I\'d like to think it over.', o: ['I\'d think it over, too, if I were the owner.', 'Of course. We can set it aside until the end of the day.', 'It\'s too heavy for me to hold up there.', 'Thinking is overrated. I\'d just buy it.'], a: 1, x: '« Hold » (mettre de côté) + « think it over » (réfléchir). La bonne réponse accepte. Les autres répètent « hold » et « think ».' },
    { id: 'lzb094', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'The transfer should clear in two to three business days.', o: ['Great, I\'ll clear my desk by Friday.', 'Two or three is too many. I\'ll wait one more.', 'No, I transferred departments two weeks ago.', 'Business days? So it won\'t land until after the weekend.'], a: 3, x: '« Clear » = être crédité ; « business days » exclut le week-end. La bonne réponse en tire la bonne inférence. Les pièges changent le sens de « clear » et « transfer ».' },
    { id: 'lzb095', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I wouldn\'t put all my eggs in one basket, if I were you, especially with a market this jumpy.', o: ['I don\'t keep eggs in baskets. I prefer cartons.', 'Then I\'ll go all in on a single stock, as you suggest.', 'True. I\'m planning to spread it across a few funds.', 'I wouldn\'t put my eggs in the market either. They spoil.'], a: 2, x: 'Conseil de diversification (idiome). La bonne réponse l\'accepte. A et C sont littéraux ; B comprend le contraire.' },
    { id: 'lzb096', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'This is a steal at that price, unless I\'m missing something.', o: ['Check the reviews first. If it sounds too good to be true, it usually is.', 'Who stole it? We\'d better call the police.', 'Missing something? I haven\'t lost anything today.', 'You\'re missing a pair of shoes, I think.'], a: 0, x: '« A steal » = une affaire. « Unless I\'m missing something » invite à la prudence. La bonne réponse donne un conseil. Les pièges sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb097', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m not at death\'s door, but I\'m hardly the picture of health.', o: ['Good to hear it, so you\'ll be in on Monday as usual.', 'Pictures of health are the best sort of decoration.', 'Then take it easy this week and let me worry about the deadlines.', 'Hardly anyone\'s at death\'s door these days, thankfully.'], a: 2, x: 'Euphémisme : « hardly the picture of health » = pas en forme du tout. La bonne réponse est attentionnée. A l\'ignore ; B et C sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb098', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'The wait at the walk-in clinic was two hours, and then they sent me home with a leaflet.', o: ['The clinic walks home at about five.', 'At least they sent you home with something nice to eat.', 'Walk-in means you can use the door without a key.', 'Two hours for a leaflet. That\'s pretty underwhelming.'], a: 3, x: 'Plainte → réponse empathique. Les pièges jouent sur « walk-in » ou comprennent mal « leaflet ».' },
    { id: 'lzb099', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m not trying to be difficult, but a complimentary breakfast shouldn\'t cost fifteen dollars.', o: ['It\'s complimentary, so the fifteen dollars is just a compliment.', 'You\'re quite right. That\'s a mistake on the bill, and I\'ll remove it.', 'Fifteen dollars is quite reasonable for a full breakfast.', 'Thank you. We\'re glad you complimented the chef.'], a: 1, x: 'Complimentary = gratuit (≠ compliment). A et C confondent les mots ; B contredit sans rien résoudre. Seule la première réponse reconnaît l\'erreur.' },
    { id: 'lzb100', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'My card was declined at the till this morning, which was embarrassing, given that I\'d just been paid.', o: ['Let me check. Sometimes a fraud alert freezes a card after a large deposit.', 'Don\'t be embarrassed. Everyone declines something now and then.', 'I was paid too, but at a different till.', 'I\'d decline the offer, too, if I were you.'], a: 0, x: '« Decline » (carte refusée) ≠ refuser une offre. La bonne réponse propose une explication plausible. Les pièges jouent sur « decline » et « paid ».' },
    { id: 'lzb101', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m afraid this flight is oversold, so we\'re asking for volunteers to take a later one.', o: ['What would I get in return? I\'m not in a rush.', 'Don\'t worry, I\'m sure the flight is safe.', 'It\'s sold out, so I\'ll have to take the bus.', 'I volunteered at the airline last summer.'], a: 0, x: '« Oversold » = surréservé. La bonne réponse négocie une compensation. A confond avec la peur ; B et C ne répondent pas.' },
    { id: 'lzb102', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'You\'ll want to read the fine print before you sign anything.', o: ['Yes, the print is in a very fine font, thanks.', 'I signed it last week, and the ink has dried.', 'Fine, I\'ll print it in color, then.', 'I will. Last time, hidden fees caught me off guard.'], a: 3, x: '« Fine print » = petits caractères d\'un contrat. La bonne réponse montre qu\'on a compris le conseil. Les pièges répètent « fine » et « print ».' },
    { id: 'lzb103', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'The manager wants everyone to submit their timesheets by noon.', o: ['He submitted a request for a new desk.', 'It\'s almost noon. Let\'s have lunch.', 'Noon today? I haven\'t even filled mine in yet.', 'I\'ll sign the sheet, but on Monday I\'m off.'], a: 2, x: 'La bonne réponse réagit à l\'information (délai serré). Les autres répètent « submit », « noon » ou « sheet » sans lien logique.' },
    { id: 'lzb104', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ve had this nagging pain in my lower back since I moved the sofa.', o: ['I moved the sofa to the lower floor last week.', 'Sounds like you pulled something. Maybe you should see a physio.', 'Nagging is what my mother does, too.', 'Back pain is common after a long drive.'], a: 1, x: '« Nagging » = tenace. La bonne réponse relie la cause et la solution. Les autres répètent « move », « back » sans conseil.' },
    { id: 'lzb105', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'Does this medication come with any side effects I should know about?', o: ['The effects are on the side of the box.', 'It comes with a leaflet and a receipt.', 'Medication should be taken sooner rather than later.', 'Some people feel drowsy, so avoid driving until you know how it affects you.'], a: 3, x: 'Question sur les effets secondaires → une information précise. Les pièges répètent « come with », « side » sans donner d\'effets.' },
    { id: 'lzb106', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Everything in this shop is marked up by at least fifty percent.', o: ['No wonder it\'s empty. Let\'s try the outlet down the road.', 'Fifty percent? Then I\'ll take two, since they\'re cheap.', 'Fifty percent is half the price, I suppose.', 'Yes, the marks on the wall are very high up.'], a: 0, x: '« Marked up » = prix majorés. La bonne réponse en déduit une conséquence. Les pièges répètent « mark ».' },
    { id: 'lzb107', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Is there any chance of exchanging this without the receipt?', o: ['Yes, the receipt is in the bag.', 'We can do store credit, but I\'ll need to see some form of ID.', 'There\'s no chance of rain this afternoon.', 'I exchanged it with a friend last year.'], a: 1, x: 'Question sur une possibilité (« any chance ») → réponse avec condition. Les autres répètent « receipt » ou « chance » hors sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb108', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'Hi, I ordered a medium sweater, but a small one arrived.', o: ['Mediums are more popular in the winter.', 'That one is on sale this week.', 'I\'m sorry about that. I can send out a replacement right away.', 'Yes, I\'ll order a small one for you.'], a: 2, x: 'Plainte sur une erreur de commande → excuse et remplacement. C inverse le sens de la demande.' },
    { id: 'lzb109', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'Are you seeing anyone at the moment, or are you still playing the field?', o: ['I see my doctor at the moment, thanks.', 'Football is my favorite field sport.', 'Nobody serious. I\'m enjoying being on my own for now.', 'Yes, I\'m seeing the film at the moment, too.'], a: 2, x: '« See someone » = sortir avec quelqu\'un ; « play the field » = papillonner. Les pièges prennent ces expressions au sens littéral.' },
    { id: 'lzb110', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'Look, I don\'t want to make a fuss, but this is the third time I\'ve had to chase this refund.', o: ['You\'re absolutely right to be frustrated, and I\'ll escalate it today.', 'No fuss at all. The refund counter is on the third floor.', 'I\'m afraid we don\'t chase customers for payment here.', 'Third time lucky, as they say. Enjoy your refund!'], a: 0, x: '« I don\'t want to make a fuss, but… » introduit une vraie plainte. La bonne réponse reconnaît le problème et propose une action. C contresens : le remboursement n\'a pas eu lieu.' },
    { id: 'lzb111', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'How often should I take these tablets?', o: ['They\'re white and rather small.', 'I took them yesterday, with water.', 'The pharmacy is open until nine.', 'Twice a day, with food.'], a: 3, x: '« How often » → fréquence. Les autres parlent de l\'apparence, du passé ou de l\'horaire.' },
    { id: 'lzb112', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'It\'s hardly the Ritz, but the room is clean and the staff are lovely.', o: ['The Ritz is much further along this street.', 'That\'s all I need. I\'m only there to sleep, anyway.', 'Lovely, I\'ll book the Ritz, then, since it\'s hardly more.', 'Hardly anyone stays at the Ritz these days.'], a: 1, x: '« Hardly the Ritz » = pas luxueux. La bonne réponse accepte les compromis. Les pièges répètent « Ritz » et « hardly ».' },
    { id: 'lzb113', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'The train\'s running about twenty minutes behind, but they\'re not saying why.', o: ['Typical. They never tell us anything until we\'re already stuck.', 'Behind is where I left my umbrella.', 'Twenty minutes is how long it takes to get here.', 'I\'d say so. Why train when you can drive?'], a: 0, x: '« Run behind » = avoir du retard. La bonne réponse partage la frustration. Les pièges jouent sur « behind » et « train ».' },
    { id: 'lzb114', diff: 3, g: 'W', p: 'I\'m trying to cut down on sugar, but it\'s harder than I thought.', o: ['Then you should try cutting down on exercise instead.', 'Tell me about it. Try swapping soda for sparkling water. It helped me.', 'Sugar is mostly grown in warm countries.', 'Yes, I thought it was harder than the exam.'], a: 1, x: '« Tell me about it » = je sais bien. La bonne réponse partage l\'expérience. Les pièges sont littéraux ou hors sujet.' },
    { id: 'lzb115', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'Her passport had barely a month left on it, so they wouldn\'t let her board, which, frankly, was on her.', o: ['Yes, she boarded the plane after the passport check.', 'So it was their fault, then. She should sue the airline.', 'I suppose so, though a warning at check-in would have been kind.', 'A month is plenty, so the airline made an error.'], a: 2, x: '« Was on her » = c\'était sa faute. La bonne réponse nuance. A, B et C comprennent mal (littéral, ou la faute de la compagnie).' },
    { id: 'lzb116', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'The landlord has been promising to fix that leak since the spring. I\'ll believe it when I see it.', o: ['Seeing is believing, so you can borrow my glasses.', 'The leak is fixed. I saw it for myself in spring.', 'Spring is the best season for fixing things.', 'He\'s all talk, then. Have you thought about putting it in writing?'], a: 3, x: '« I\'ll believe it when I see it » = je ne le crois pas. La bonne réponse comprend le scepticisme. Les pièges sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb117', diff: 3, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ve been on hold for forty minutes, and nobody seems to know anything.', o: ['That\'s quite all right. I enjoy the music.', 'Forty minutes is the usual wait on Mondays.', 'Forty minutes? You must have the wrong number, then.', 'I do apologize, sir. Let me see what I can do about it myself.'], a: 3, x: 'Le client se plaint : la réponse du conseiller doit s\'excuser et agir. A (registre inadapté), B (justification froide) et C (accusation) ne règlent rien.' },
    { id: 'lzb118', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'Excuse me, does this ferry stop at both islands?', o: ['It does. The first stop is the north island, then the south one.', 'Yes, I\'ve been to both islands twice.', 'The ferry leaves at the bottom of the hour.', 'Stopping is not allowed on the deck.'], a: 0, x: 'Question oui/non → confirmation + détail. Les autres répètent « islands », « ferry », « stop ».' },
    { id: 'lzb119', diff: 2, g: 'M', p: 'I need to buy a gift for my sister\'s birthday, but I\'ve no idea what she likes.', o: ['Her birthday is next week.', 'I like gifts that come in boxes.', 'How about a book or a scarf? Something you can\'t go wrong with.', 'Yes, my sister buys me presents, too.'], a: 2, x: 'Demande de conseil indirecte → suggestions. Les autres répètent « gift », « sister ».' },
    { id: 'lzb120', diff: 4, g: 'M', p: 'I\'ll hand it to you: you drove a hard bargain with that dealer.', o: ['Hand it over, then. I\'d like to see the contract.', 'Thanks. He caved in after I threatened to walk out.', 'I never bargain with dealers. I pay the listed price.', 'The dealer hands out many bargains every day.'], a: 1, x: '« I\'ll hand it to you » = chapeau ; « drive a hard bargain » = négocier durement. La bonne réponse remercie et raconte. Les pièges sont littéraux.' },
    { id: 'lzb121', diff: 4, g: 'W', p: 'I hope you don\'t mind my asking, but you wouldn\'t be heading downtown by any chance, would you?', o: ['Downtown parking is impossible, whatever the chance.', 'Not at all. I\'ve been asking myself the same question all morning.', 'As a matter of fact, I am. Hop in, and I\'ll drop you at Grand Street.', 'I don\'t mind, but I\'d rather not ask the driver.'], a: 2, x: 'Demande très indirecte et hésitante. La bonne réponse accepte avec chaleur. Les autres répètent « mind », « ask » sans répondre.' },
    { id: 'lzb122', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'What\'s the interest rate on your standard savings account?', o: ['I save about two hundred dollars a month.', 'The interest in the account was a surprise.', 'Yes, I\'ve opened a standard account.', 'It\'s currently two point five percent, and it\'s variable.'], a: 3, x: '« What\'s the rate » → un chiffre. Les autres répètent « savings », « account » sans donner de taux.' },
    { id: 'lzb123', diff: 2, g: 'W', p: 'Customs held us up for over an hour.', o: ['Yes, customs are an old tradition there.', 'An hour! Did they say why?', 'We were held in a hotel by the airport.', 'An hour is about how long the film takes.'], a: 1, x: '« Hold up » = retarder. La bonne réponse réagit avec intérêt. Les pièges reprennent « customs » (coutumes) et « hour ».' }
  );
})();
